From 71255dbfb268b624271a160c3588fcca754c4879 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 01:50:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 41/43] WIP: ARM: dts: meson-s4-s905y4-khadas-vim1s: set the
 VDDCPU boot voltage

BL2 leaves VDDCPU at its 799mV power-up value, which the vendor OPP
table only allows up to 1.404GHz, and nothing raises it for the clock
the CPUs run at once Linux starts, so the cores are undervolted and
Linux crashes at random early in boot. 959mV written by hand from the
U-Boot prompt was stable.

Set 939mV, which keeps a wide margin over the 869mV the vendor uses at
1.608GHz and is the closest voltage to the tested value whose duty
cycle reads back from the 36 count PWM period as an exact entry of the
Linux voltage table, so the kernel regulator can take over without
reprogramming VDDCPU.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm/dts/meson-s4-s905y4-khadas-vim1s-u-boot.dtsi | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm/dts/meson-s4-s905y4-khadas-vim1s-u-boot.dtsi b/arch/arm/dts/meson-s4-s905y4-khadas-vim1s-u-boot.dtsi
index 0d233fd63e3..a19dd045f84 100644
--- a/arch/arm/dts/meson-s4-s905y4-khadas-vim1s-u-boot.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/dts/meson-s4-s905y4-khadas-vim1s-u-boot.dtsi
@@ -62,3 +62,7 @@
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+&vddcpu {
+	regulator-init-microvolt = <939000>;
+};
